    <!-- ================= PROBLEM ================= -->
    <section id="problem">
      <div class="section-tag mono">01 — motivation</div>
      <h2>The problem, with evidence</h2>
      <p>Legacy modernization tools are good at syntax translation and bad at preserving intent. A COBOL validation branch, an implicit exception path, an off-by-one that was actually load-bearing — these don't show up as syntax errors when they're dropped. The modernized code compiles, runs, and returns plausible values. It just quietly disagrees with the original on some slice of inputs. Nothing in a normal test suite or code review is built to catch that, because nobody wrote a test for the behavior nobody knew was there.</p>
      <p>Two recent papers make this concrete rather than anecdotal.</p>

      <h3>Finding 1 — self-review doesn't work</h3>
      <p>A 2026 study run independently of any LLM vendor (with authors noting the work was conducted outside their employer's data and systems) tested whether a model can be trusted to judge its own migration output. Across <strong>1,980 modernization calls spanning 11 production LLMs from 7 model families</strong>, snippets engineered to contain a real semantic trap — numeric semantics, lazy evaluation, type-model differences between legacy and modern runtimes — drifted in <strong>39.7% of attempts</strong>, versus <strong>7.0%</strong> on a matched benign-control set that needed no real modernization at all. That gap alone shows drift is concentrated, not noise.</p>
      <p>The more damning number: when each model was then asked to review its <em>own</em> output for behavioral drift, self-review missed <strong>31.7%</strong> of the drift it had just introduced — and per-model behavior was strongly bimodal, including cases where a model correctly described the exact semantic difference between the old and new code in its reasoning, then still declared the migration behavior-preserving.</p>
      <div class="callout warn">
        <p><strong>Implication for this project:</strong> asking the migration agent (or a second instance of the same model family) to grade its own diff is not a verification strategy. The checker has to be a separate system with no stake in the answer — deterministic where possible, and blind to which side of the diff is "the AI's."</p>
      </div>

      <h3>Finding 2 — the fix that exists still has a code-generation gap</h3>
      <p>A parallel line of work, AgentModernize, treats modernization as a behavior-preservation problem rather than a translation problem. It splits the job across four specialized agents — a legacy analyzer, a specification generator, a modernization transformer, and an equivalence validator — connected through an intermediate artifact called a Behavioral Specification Graph (BSG): a directed graph of operations with explicit preconditions, postconditions, and invariants, generated <em>before</em> any modern code is written, so the extracted business logic is inspectable rather than implicit.</p>
      <p>That framing is the right one, and this project borrows it. But it still depends on an LLM to close the loop — extracting the graph, generating code from it, and validating equivalence via generated tests and differential trace analysis. Later replication work found the reported gains were sensitive to model backbone and prompting: a full pipeline with feedback beat a single-prompt baseline on a weaker backbone, but the crossover reversed on stronger, more recent backbones. In other words, the BSG approach reduces the search space for errors but doesn't eliminate the need for an independent, non-LLM-graded check on the result.</p>

      <div class="chart-wrap">
        <canvas id="driftChart" height="110"></canvas>
      </div>
      <p style="font-size:13px;color:var(--muted);margin-top:-10px;">Independently measured drift and self-review miss rates. Source: "Articulate but Wrong" (arXiv:2605.21537), n=660 per condition for the drift comparison.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <p>This project doesn't try to build a better migration agent, and it doesn't try to out-prompt AgentModernize. It builds the layer that sits in front of <em>any</em> migration agent — including a good one — and answers one question with evidence instead of an opinion: <strong>did the behavior actually stay the same?</strong></p>
      </div>
    </section>

