      <h2>Scope: v1 boundaries</h2>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div>
          <h4>In scope for v1</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>One legacy pattern, chosen from real production code you've written — not a toy example.</li>
            <li>Property-based input generation covering every branch of that one function.</li>
            <li>A deterministic, containerized dual-run harness for legacy vs. modern code.</li>
            <li>A diff engine that classifies outcomes without any AI involvement.</li>
            <li>One AI migration agent wired in last, scored by the harness already proven to work without it.</li>
            <li>A CI gate (GitHub Action) that fails a PR on detected drift.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4>Explicitly out of scope for v1</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Repository-scale migration (multiple files, cross-module state, shared databases).</li>
            <li>Automatic BSG-style extraction of business rules from source — the pattern is hand-specified in v1.</li>
            <li>Multi-language support beyond one legacy/modern pair (e.g. one Express route pattern).</li>
            <li>A general-purpose "trust score" for arbitrary LLM output — this is scoped to code-behavior equivalence only.</li>
            <li>Automatic remediation. V1 flags and reports; it does not attempt to fix drift.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout verify">
        <p>The ordering of the checkpoints is deliberate: the harness and diff engine are built and trusted <em>before</em> any AI is introduced. If step 5 is your first exposure to whether the system works, you've built a demo, not a verification engine.</p>
      </div>
