    <!-- ================= CONCEPTS ================= -->
    <section id="concepts">
      <div class="section-tag mono">04 — foundations</div>
      <h2>Core concepts</h2>

      <h3>The pattern spec</h3>
      <p>Every run starts from a single artifact: the pattern spec. It names the legacy function, its expected modern equivalent, the input domain, and any known invariants (e.g. "response status is always 400 or 422 on validation failure, never 500"). This is the only hand-authored part of the pipeline, and it's what checkpoint 1 produces.</p>
      <pre><code>// pattern-spec.ts
export const validateOrderForm: PatternSpec = {
  id: "validate-order-form-v1",
  legacy: { file: "legacy/orderValidation.js", export: "validateOrderForm" },
  modern: { file: "modern/orderValidation.ts", export: "validateOrderForm" },
  inputShape: OrderFormArbitrary,   // fast-check arbitrary, see checkpoint 2
  invariants: [
    "never throws an unhandled exception",
    "returns { valid: boolean, errors: string[] }",
    "errors is non-empty iff valid is false",
  ],
};</code></pre>

      <h3>Three-way behavioral classification</h3>
      <p>The diff engine never returns a single boolean. Two outputs can be textually different and still be behaviorally equivalent — a reordered error array, an ISO string vs. a Date object carrying the same instant. Collapsing that distinction into pass/fail either drowns you in false positives or hides real drift behind a normalizer that's too forgiving.</p>
      <table>
        <tr><th>Classification</th><th>Meaning</th><th>CI action</th></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">exact_match</td><td>Byte-identical structured output for a given input.</td><td>pass</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">equivalent_shape</td><td>Output differs syntactically but is provably equivalent under the pattern's declared equivalence rules (e.g. key ordering, type coercion the spec allows).</td><td>pass, logged</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">drift</td><td>Output differs in a way not covered by any declared equivalence rule, or an invariant is violated.</td><td><strong>fail</strong>, blocks merge</td></tr>
      </table>

      <h3>Property-based generation vs. hand-written examples</h3>
      <p>Hand-written test cases encode the branches you remembered to think of. Property-based generation, using fast-check, defines the <em>shape</em> of valid and invalid input space and lets the tool search it — including the boundary values, empty collections, unicode edge cases, and malformed nesting a human tester skips on the fifth example. For this project, coverage is measured against the legacy function's actual branch count, not against an arbitrary input quota.</p>
    </section>

