      <div class="section-tag mono">06 — execution</div>
      <h2>Build plan — 5 checkpoints over 3–4 weeks</h2>
      <p>Each checkpoint has a hard exit criterion taken directly from the project brief. Don't move to the next checkpoint until the current one's criterion is objectively true — this is a verification tool; it should hold itself to the same standard.</p>

      <div class="chart-wrap">
        <canvas id="timelineChart" height="150"></canvas>
      </div>

      <div class="checkpoint">
        <div class="checkpoint-head"><span class="cp-num">CP-1</span><h3>Pick one real pattern</h3></div>
        <div class="checkpoint-body">
          <p>Choose a single legacy pattern you've actually built and understand at the level of "I know why that weird branch is there." Conditional form-validation logic is the brief's suggested example: it has clear branches, clear invariants, and clear failure modes, without needing a database or network call to reproduce.</p>
          <p><strong>Work:</strong></p>
          <ul>
            <li>Extract the function into a standalone module with no external dependencies beyond what's needed to run it headless.</li>
            <li>Write the pattern spec (legacy input shape → expected modern output shape) as a typed object, not a comment.</li>
            <li>Enumerate every branch and exception path by hand once, as a checklist — this becomes your coverage target for checkpoint 2, and your invariant list.</li>
            <li>Write down, explicitly, what "equivalent but differently shaped" is allowed to mean for this function (e.g. error array order doesn't matter; error message wording does).</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="done-bar">✓ Exit criterion: the pattern is written down as legacy input + expected modern output, with an explicit invariant and equivalence-rule list.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="checkpoint">
        <div class="checkpoint-head"><span class="cp-num">CP-2</span><h3>Build input generation</h3></div>
        <div class="checkpoint-body">
          <p>Use fast-check to define arbitraries that describe the input domain — not a list of examples, a generator that can produce and shrink examples. Shrinking matters here specifically: when the diff engine later finds drift, you want the smallest input that reproduces it, not the 4KB random blob that happened to trigger it first.</p>
          <pre><code>import fc from "fast-check";

const OrderFormArbitrary = fc.record({
  customerEmail: fc.oneof(fc.emailAddress(), fc.string()), // valid + malformed
  quantity: fc.oneof(fc.integer({ min: -10, max: 10000 }), fc.constant(NaN)),
  couponCode: fc.option(fc.string({ maxLength: 12 }), { nil: undefined }),
  shippingCountry: fc.constantFrom("US", "CA", "GB", "XX", ""), // XX/"" = invalid
});

// Coverage check against your CP-1 branch checklist, not just a raw count:
fc.assert(
  fc.property(OrderFormArbitrary, (input) => {
    branchTracker.record(legacyValidateOrderForm, input);
  }),
  { numRuns: 500 }
);
// numRuns tuned upward until branchTracker reports 100% of the CP-1 checklist hit</code></pre>
          <div class="done-bar">✓ Exit criterion: 100+ generated inputs, and every branch on the checkpoint-1 checklist is confirmed hit by at least one generated case.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="checkpoint">
        <div class="checkpoint-head"><span class="cp-num">CP-3</span><h3>Build the dual-run harness</h3></div>
        <div class="checkpoint-body">
          <p>Legacy and modern code run in separate, disposable Docker containers, each fed the identical generated corpus, each logging structured output for every input — including thrown exceptions, which are a result, not a crash of the harness.</p>
          <pre><code>// harness/runInContainer.ts (shape, not full impl)
type RunResult =
  | { kind: "value"; input: unknown; output: unknown; ms: number }
  | { kind: "error"; input: unknown; errorName: string; message: string; ms: number };

async function runBatch(image: string, entrypoint: string, corpus: unknown[]): Promise<RunResult[]> {
  const container = await docker.run(image, {
    Cmd: ["node", entrypoint],
    HostConfig: { NetworkMode: "none", Memory: 256 * 1024 * 1024 },
  });
  // corpus is piped in over stdin as NDJSON; results streamed back the same way
  return collectResults(container, corpus);
}</code></pre>
          <p><strong>Isolation checklist:</strong> no network access from either container, fixed and identical Node.js version pinned in both images, no shared filesystem mounts, wall-clock timeout per input so an infinite loop in either version fails closed rather than hanging CI.</p>
          <div class="done-bar">✓ Exit criterion: both versions run headless in containers and log structured output — including errors — for every generated input.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="checkpoint">
        <div class="checkpoint-head"><span class="cp-num">CP-4</span><h3>Build the diff engine</h3></div>
        <div class="checkpoint-body">
          <p>Pure, deterministic, no AI. This is the component you'll actually trust, so it needs to be the easiest one to audit. See the dedicated section below for the full classification logic and pseudocode.</p>
          <p><strong>Validation for this checkpoint specifically:</strong> before trusting the diff engine on real agent output, prove it on two synthetic cases —</p>
          <ul>
            <li>A deliberately broken "migration" (e.g. delete one validation branch) → must be flagged as drift, with the specific input that exposes it surfaced in the report.</li>
            <li>A correct hand-migration → must pass clean, with zero false-positive drift across the full corpus.</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="done-bar">✓ Exit criterion: the deliberately-broken migration is flagged, and the correct migration passes clean — both on the same generated corpus.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="checkpoint">
        <div class="checkpoint-head"><span class="cp-num">CP-5</span><h3>Wire in an AI migration agent</h3></div>
        <div class="checkpoint-body">
          <p>Only now does an LLM enter the system, and only as a source of candidate code — never as a judge of its own output. The agent receives the legacy source and the pattern spec (input shape, invariants, equivalence rules) and returns a migration attempt, which is scored by the exact harness and diff engine already proven in checkpoints 3–4.</p>
          <pre><code>// agent/migrate.ts
async function migrate(legacySource: string, spec: PatternSpec): Promise<string> {
  const res = await anthropic.messages.create({
    model: "claude-sonnet-4-6",
    max_tokens: 4000,
    messages: [{
      role: "user",
      content: `Migrate this legacy function to modern TypeScript.
Preserve every branch and invariant listed below exactly; do not simplify control flow.

Legacy source:
${legacySource}

Declared invariants:
${spec.invariants.join("\\n")}`,
    }],
  });
  return extractCode(res.content);
}
// Output goes straight into the CP-3 harness. The harness and diff engine
// do not know or care that this candidate came from an LLM.</code></pre>
          <div class="done-bar">✓ Exit criterion: one full run completes end to end — legacy in, agent migrates, harness scores it, verdict comes out — with a real, non-trivial verdict (not just a smoke test).</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= DIFF ENGINE ================= -->
    <section id="diffengine">
      <div class="section-tag mono">07 — core logic</div>
      <h2>The diff engine, in detail</h2>
      <p>This is the trust boundary of the whole project, so its decision logic is laid out explicitly rather than left as "compare the outputs."</p>

      <div class="diagram-frame">
        <div class="mermaid">
flowchart TD
  S["For each input in corpus:<br/>legacy_result, modern_result"] --> Q1{"Both threw?"}
  Q1 -- yes --> Q1a{"Same error class<br/>and declared-equivalent message?"}
  Q1a -- yes --> R1["exact_match"]
  Q1a -- no --> R4["drift: divergent failure mode"]
  Q1 -- one threw, one didn't --> R5["drift: failure-mode mismatch"]
  Q1 -- neither threw --> Q2{"Structurally<br/>identical (deep-eq)?"}
  Q2 -- yes --> R1b["exact_match"]
  Q2 -- no --> Q3{"Matches a declared<br/>equivalence rule from<br/>the pattern spec?"}
  Q3 -- yes --> R2["equivalent_shape<br/>(logged, not a failure)"]
  Q3 -- no --> R3["drift: unexplained value difference"]
  R1 --> INV{"Invariant check<br/>on this input"}
  R1b --> INV
  R2 --> INV
  INV -- violated --> R6["drift: invariant violated<br/>(overrides prior classification)"]
  INV -- holds --> DONE["record classification"]
  R3 --> DONE
  R4 --> DONE
  R5 --> DONE
  R6 --> DONE
  DONE --> AGG["Aggregate across corpus"]
  AGG --> V{"Any drift cases?"}
  V -- yes --> FAIL["Verdict: FAIL<br/>list minimal reproducing inputs"]
  V -- no --> PASS["Verdict: PASS<br/>(+ equivalent_shape log)"]

  classDef ok fill:#E4EFE8,stroke:#1E6E52,color:#171C1B;
  classDef bad fill:#F3E5DD,stroke:#A6431F,color:#171C1B;
  class R1,R1b,R2,PASS ok;
  class R3,R4,R5,R6,FAIL bad;
        </div>
        <div class="cap">Fig. 3 — Invariant checks run after equivalence classification and can override it: an output can match the modern shape exactly and still fail if it breaks a stated invariant (e.g. throwing where the spec guarantees it never will).</div>
      </div>

      <h3>Why exceptions get first-class treatment</h3>
      <p>The single most common way an AI migration silently changes behavior is in the exception path: a legacy branch that returned a typed error object gets migrated to code that throws instead, or vice versa. Both can look identical on the happy path across dozens of generated inputs and only disagree on the handful of edge cases that hit that branch — which is exactly why property-based generation in checkpoint 2 has to specifically target boundary and malformed inputs, not just realistic ones.</p>

      <h3>Reporting a drift case</h3>
      <p>Every drift entry in the report carries: the minimal reproducing input (after fast-check shrinking), the legacy output, the modern output, the specific classification reason, and — when the candidate came from an agent — the model and prompt version used, so drift patterns can be tracked per model over time the same way the "Articulate but Wrong" study tracked them per model family.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= CI ================= -->
    <section id="ci">
      <div class="section-tag mono">08 — delivery</div>
      <h2>CI/CD integration</h2>
      <p>The CI gate's job is narrow: run the verification engine against the diff in a pull request, and fail the check if the diff engine returns any drift classification. It does not re-run the AI agent unless the PR is specifically requesting a fresh migration.</p>

      <div class="diagram-frame">
        <div class="mermaid">
flowchart LR
  PR["Pull request opened<br/>or updated"] --> T{"Migration<br/>pattern changed?"}
  T -- no --> SKIP["Skip: no verification needed"]
  T -- yes --> RUN["Run verify workflow"]
  RUN --> BUILD["Build legacy + modern<br/>Docker images"]
  BUILD --> EXEC["Run harness against<br/>stored corpus for this pattern"]
  EXEC --> CLASSIFY["Diff engine classifies<br/>all results"]
  CLASSIFY --> G{"Any drift?"}
  G -- yes --> BLOCK["Check: failed<br/>PR comment with drift cases"]
  G -- no --> ALLOW["Check: passed<br/>PR comment with coverage summary"]

  classDef bad fill:#F3E5DD,stroke:#A6431F,color:#171C1B;
  classDef ok fill:#E4EFE8,stroke:#1E6E52,color:#171C1B;
  class BLOCK bad;
  class ALLOW ok;
        </div>
        <div class="cap">Fig. 4 — The corpus is generated once per pattern and stored (not regenerated per PR run), so results are reproducible across runs and reviewers can diff two CI runs against the exact same inputs.</div>
      </div>

      <pre><code>## .github/workflows/verify.yml
name: behavioral-verification
on:
  pull_request:
    paths:
      - "patterns/**"
      - "modern/**"

jobs:
  verify:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4
        with: { node-version: "20" }
      - run: npm ci
      - name: Build legacy + modern images
        run: npm run bve:build-images
      - name: Run verification for changed patterns
        run: npm run bve:verify -- --changed-only --report=json
      - name: Publish report
        if: always()
        run: npm run bve:report -- --format=github-comment
      - name: Enforce gate
        run: npm run bve:gate  # exits 1 on any drift classification</code></pre>
    </section>

