    <!-- ================= RISKS ================= -->
    <section id="risks">
      <div class="section-tag mono">11 — critique</div>
      <h2>Risks &amp; open questions</h2>
      <table>
        <tr><th>Risk</th><th>Severity</th><th>Mitigation</th></tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Equivalence rules become a dumping ground — every drift gets "explained" into <span class="mono">equivalent_shape</span> to make CI pass.</td>
          <td><span class="risk-cell risk-high">high</span></td>
          <td>Equivalence rules are declared per-pattern in the spec, reviewed like code, and every use is logged in the report — not silently absorbed.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Property-based generation gives false confidence if the arbitrary's domain doesn't actually match production input reality.</td>
          <td><span class="risk-cell risk-med">medium</span></td>
          <td>Branch-coverage check against the CP-1 checklist is a hard gate, not a nice-to-have; seed the corpus with real production edge cases where available.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Container startup latency makes the harness too slow for a tight PR feedback loop as more patterns are added.</td>
          <td><span class="risk-cell risk-med">medium</span></td>
          <td><span class="mono">changed-only</span> filtering in CI (Fig. 4); container image caching; batch multiple inputs per container invocation instead of one container per input.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Single-pattern v1 doesn't prove the approach generalizes across pattern types (state machines, async flows, DB-touching code).</td>
          <td><span class="risk-cell risk-med">medium</span></td>
          <td>Explicitly scoped out of v1; roadmap item for v2 (see below), chosen deliberately rather than by accident.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Non-determinism in the legacy or modern code itself (e.g. <span class="mono">Date.now()</span>, random IDs) produces false drift.</td>
          <td><span class="risk-cell risk-low">low</span></td>
          <td>Clock and RNG injection points identified during CP-1 extraction; harness fixes both to deterministic values inside the containers.</td>
        </tr>
      </table>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= METRICS ================= -->
    <section id="metrics">
      <div class="section-tag mono">12 — evaluation</div>
      <h2>Success metrics</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Detection:</strong> the deliberately-broken migration from checkpoint 4 is caught on 100% of the runs it's tested against — this is a correctness bar, not a target to trend toward.</li>
        <li><strong>False-positive rate:</strong> a correct hand-migration produces zero drift classifications across the full generated corpus, repeated across at least 3 independent corpus regenerations.</li>
        <li><strong>Coverage:</strong> 100% of the checkpoint-1 branch checklist is exercised by the generated corpus, tracked automatically rather than eyeballed.</li>
        <li><strong>Agent evaluation (checkpoint 5+):</strong> drift rate per model/prompt combination, reported the way the self-review study reports it — broken out by trap category, not collapsed into one aggregate pass rate.</li>
        <li><strong>CI latency:</strong> a verification run for one pattern completes within a budget that keeps it usable as a real PR gate (target: under 3 minutes including image build, adjusted once real numbers exist).</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

